1. Working with forms

We often have data like comments, revuews, rating which has to be generated by our users of our webpage.
In such scenarios we need to expose forms for our users and we need to handle them.
We learned how to build forms from scratch in html and how to handle it from scratch in our views.py, by manually validating, by manually extracting the data and then by manually using that data.
Whilst that is possible and sometimes that might be enough, it can be cumbersome to do that all on your own.
That's why we have this "Form" class provided by Django which we can inherit (class ReviewForm(forms.Form):) which allows us to build our own form class where we define our own form controls ('user_name', 'max_length', 'review_text', 'rating')
We can give them our own labels, validation logic and possible error messages that should be shown.
Now with such a form class we have the huge advantage that we can easily generate the HTML code that belongs to this form.
We can either generate the form as a whole or individual parts of the form like the label filed, input field, the errors part and then we can really build the template as we need it but we don't have to build all the HTML code from scratch.
But that's just one of the advantages we get when using these forms as provided by Django.
Another major advantage can be seen in our views.py. There now we don't have to manually pull out the data and validate the data and do something with it.
Instead when using Form class inheriting from Django.form we can just past the received data to that form class to instantiate this class, and then we ca call methods like "is_valid" to easily validate all the data with the rules we configured when we configured the form class.
That then allows us to easily render and return different responses based on the validity and if the data is valid we also we have easy access to the cleaned data, for example to then store that in one of our models and thenfore in our database.
But if that's our usecase, getting data from one of our models, then we can simplify this even more.
Because for this, Django gives us this ModelForm instead of the regular Form from which we can inherit.
when doing that we still get a Form class which we can use, but we don't even need to define our controls there.
Instead we just tell Django which controls, which fields from our model we want to have as controls (all, some selected lists of fields or maybe all fields but some excluded ones)
We can still configure them regarding label or error messages but then we have Django infer our form controls from our model, we can still output thos form controls in the template and when it comes to saving that data, we can still validate it as we used to but we don't have to create a new instance of our model now and pass the form data into it instead we can call .save() on the form itself if it's based on such a model form and the it will automaticaly save it to the database, eigther as a brand new data entry or if we set that "instance" keyword argument and we point at an existing instance of our model it will then update, so update that data entry in tha database with the form data.
And then we have the class based views which can replace the view functions. The class based views are inherited from Django's built it View templates so to say, View blueprints.
With that you can inherit from the generic View to have class where you define get() and post() methods and possible other HTTP as well and the Django will automaticaly call the correct method for you depending on what kind of request is reaching that view.
You still point at that view in our ulrs.py like this : path("", views.ReviewView.as_view())
    But then if the same url is reached with different HTTP methods, the different methods in your view will be called and you don't have to add the "if" check in your view function to find out wether it's a post() or get() request.


